# briefing.txt -- TEMPLATE, not your real briefing

This mirrors the real structure Tommy's briefing.txt uses (Parts A-I),
confirmed working with `scoring.py`. Copy this to `briefing.txt` (already
gitignored) and replace every section with real, verified content if
you're setting this system up for someone else, or need to reconstruct it.

This entire file is sent to Claude verbatim as the system prompt for every
scoring call (see docs/adr/ADR-002) -- `scoring.py` adds no formatting
instructions of its own, so Part A (rules) and Part I (output schema) below
are load-bearing, not just documentation. Keep them in the file.

---

==================================================
PART A. ROLE AND RULES
==================================================
<Tell the model what it's doing (score one posting at a time against this
candidate) and any standing rules: never invent facts, how to handle a
missing salary, what to do if the posting text is too thin, tone/wording
rules, what to do when the user message doesn't supply optional context
(e.g. contact/access info, posting date, applicant count -- give an
explicit default). End with: "Return strict JSON only, with no text before
or after it and no markdown fences.">

==================================================
PART B. VERIFIED CANDIDATE FACTS
==================================================
<Name, location, credentials, clearances, availability. Compensation
floors as HARD FILTERS -- e.g. a full-time annual floor and a separate
fractional/interim monthly floor, since these often differ. Location
preference in ranked order. Scale evidence relevant to the roles you're
targeting (revenue run, team size led). Target industries.>

==================================================
PART C. CAREER HISTORY (USE THIS WORDING)
==================================================
<Real, verified work history with exact wording you want the model to
reuse -- this becomes the source for "matching_facts" in every score.>

==================================================
PART D. SKILLS
==================================================
<Proven skills, sector familiarity, and anything that's a genuine
differentiator (e.g. applied AI/automation experience) worth an explicit
bonus in the scoring rubric.>

==================================================
PART E. HONEST GAPS (SCORE THESE DOWN WHEN A ROLE REQUIRES THEM)
==================================================
<What you don't have -- credentials, systems experience, scale. Be
specific; this is what keeps scores honest instead of inflated.>

==================================================
PART F. WORDING RULES (CLAIMS TO USE AND CLAIMS TO AVOID)
==================================================
<Exact phrases to use for sensitive/nuanced claims, and exact claims to
never make (overstatements, unconfirmed figures, claims that imply
something untrue). Reference contacts, in priority order.>

==================================================
PART G. TARGET TRACKS (RANKED BY LIKELIHOOD OF SUCCESS)
==================================================
<Each track: a name, a number (1, 2, 3...), example titles, what makes a
posting a strong fit, and the best resume version(s) for it. End with a
starter list of employers to check for openings -- name them, but don't
assume which ATS platform they use; that has to be verified separately
(see targets.yaml).>

==================================================
PART H. MATCH SCORE (0 TO 100)
==================================================
<The scoring parts, each with a point weight that sums to 100 (e.g.
requirements match, track/level fit, industry fit, location, compensation,
access, timing/competition), plus any bonus (e.g. an AI/automation bonus)
on top, capped at 100. Then HARD FILTERS: the exact, unambiguous
conditions that mean automatic rejection regardless of score (comp floor,
missing mandatory credential, company size ceiling, etc.) -- these should
line up with config/tracks.yaml's disqualifying-phrase list where they're
cheaply keyword-detectable. Then TIERS: the score bands and what each one
means for action (e.g. A = apply today, B = apply this week, C = only via
referral, below C = skip).>

==================================================
PART I. OUTPUT FORMAT (STRICT JSON)
==================================================
<The exact JSON shape you want back, as a literal example object --
scoring.py's REQUIRED_TOP_LEVEL_KEYS must match this file's top-level keys
exactly, so if you change this section, update
src/jobalerts/scoring.py::REQUIRED_TOP_LEVEL_KEYS (and score_to_db_row's
field mapping) to match. Include a short "Notes" section clarifying any
field whose meaning isn't obvious from its name (e.g. what "track" values
are valid, what "tier" values are valid, which field is set on a hard
filter failure).>
